<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luigi Carlo Farini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1812">1812</a>–<a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luigi Carlo Farini was an Italian physician, historian and statesman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Risorgimento</a>. In 1859 he became dictator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> and Parma and united them with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilia">Emilia</a>, which voted in 1860 to join the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. He later served as lieutenant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and, in 1862–1863, as prime minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luigi-carlo-farini.jpg" alt="Luigi Carlo Farini" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fotografo anonimo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luigi_Carlo_Farini.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Luigi Carlo Farini was born on 22 October 1812 at Russi, in what is now the province of Ravenna. He studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, interrupting his course to take part in the revolution of 1831, and practised as a physician at Russi and Ravenna. In 1843 his political opinions brought him under police suspicion and he was expelled from the Papal States. He lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and travelled about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> as private physician to Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome-bonaparte">Jérôme Bonaparte</a>, returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> when Pius IX began his reign with apparently liberal and nationalist tendencies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In March 1848 Farini became secretary-general to the minister of the interior, but resigned with the whole ministry on 29 April, when Pius IX refused to let his troops fight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Elected for Faenza, he served again in the Mamiani cabinet. He went into exile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> when a reactionary and priestly policy was instituted, became convinced that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> could be liberated only through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>, and wrote for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a>'s paper Il Risorgimento. His chief historical work, Lo Stato Romano dal 1815 al 1850, appeared in four volumes in 1850. He was minister of public instruction from 1851 to May 1852, and his two letters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ewart-gladstone">William Ewart Gladstone</a>, in 1856 and 1857, created a sensation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>When Francis V, duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>, was expelled in 1859, Farini was sent to the city as Piedmontese commissioner. Recalled after the peace of Villafranca, he stayed, became a Modenese citizen and dictator of the state, and negotiated an alliance with Parma, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> and Tuscany. After the plebiscite for annexation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a> appointed him minister of the interior in June 1860, and later viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. After Cavour's death in 1861 he succeeded Rattazzi as premier, but illness forced him to resign on 24 March 1863. He died in poverty in 1866 at Quarto dei Mille in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>; in 1878 his remains were moved to Russi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Luigi Carlo Farini” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…During the Second Italian War of Independence the regent left in June 1859, provisional authorities, later led by <b>Luigi Carlo Farini</b>, joined the duchy to the United Provinces of Central Italy, and a plebiscite in March 1860 confirmed annexation to the Kingdom of Sardinia, soon to become Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Luigi Carlo Farini</b> governed Modena, Parma and Romagna, united as Emilia, while Bettino Ricasoli led Tuscany.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-provinces-of-central-italy">United Provinces of Central Italy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Russi, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Russi, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.38,12.03&amp;year=1847" aria-label="Russi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Luigi Carlo Farini"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5730 3745h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5730 3745h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.38,12.03&amp;year=1847">What was here in 1847? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
